# Host-specific sway output layout.
#
# Copy to ~/.config/sway/outputs.conf and fill in your own identifiers. This
# file is NOT symlinked by dfinstall and NOT tracked in this repo — output
# identifiers embed panel serial numbers, so they are per-machine data.
# `include $HOME/.config/sway/outputs.conf` in config/sway/config picks it up;
# sway tolerates the file being absent, so a fresh machine needs no stub.
#
# Discover your identifiers with:
#     swaymsg -t get_outputs | grep -E 'make|model|serial|Output'
#
# ── Why identifiers, not connector names ──────────────────────────────────
# Match externals by IDENTIFIER ("make model serial"), not by connector name.
# A DisplayLink/evdi output's connector is handed out in attach order, so it
# enumerates as DVI-I-1 one session and DVI-I-2 the next; `output DVI-I-1 ...`
# then silently stops applying. Identifiers are stable across replugs.
# Built-in laptop panels are the exception — eDP-1 is stable, so name it
# directly.
#
# ── Vertical alignment ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Center-align panels of differing heights rather than top-aligning them, so
# their vertical midpoints share a y coordinate and the pointer crosses
# between screens at mid-height without snagging on a dead corner. For panels
# of height H whose midpoints should meet at y=M, set position y = M - H/2.
#
# ── Modes ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Omit `resolution` unless you actually need a non-preferred mode. Setting a
# resolution the panel does not advertise is silently ignored and sway falls
# back to the preferred mode, which looks like the directive "did nothing".

# Example: laptop | 27" 1440p | 27" 1080p, left to right, midpoints at y=720.

# Built-in panel, 1920x1200. Stable connector name, so no identifier needed.
output eDP-1                              position 0 120

# Center monitor, 2560x1440.
output "MAKE MODEL SERIAL"                position 1920 0

# Right monitor, 1920x1080.
output "MAKE MODEL SERIAL"                position 4480 180

# ── Which screen a workspace opens on ─────────────────────────────────────
# Geometry above is only half of a multi-monitor setup. The other half is
# `workspace <n> output <outputs...>`, which is what makes a workspace come
# back to the right screen after a re-dock, and it belongs here for the same
# reason the positions do: it names outputs.
#
# sway takes a LIST and uses the first available, so write a fallback chain
# rather than a single output — an assignment naming only the dock is inert
# on the road, and the workspace lands wherever sway felt like putting it:
#
#     workspace 1 output "MAKE MODEL SERIAL" eDP-1
#
# ⚠ Two properties of this command, both from sway(5), both load-bearing:
#
#   * It is a PREFERENCE THAT RE-ASSERTS ITSELF, not a one-time placement.
#     "If the workspace gets placed on an output further down the list and an
#     output that is higher on the list becomes available, the workspace will
#     be moved to the higher priority output." That sentence is the entire
#     reason to bother: plug the dock back in and the workspaces walk home by
#     themselves. Nothing else in this file does that.
#
#   * It "does not affect existing workspaces". So adding a line here and
#     running `swaymsg reload` looks like it did nothing — the workspaces you
#     already have stay put, and only the next one to be CREATED lands
#     correctly. To move what already exists, switch to it and
#     `move workspace to output <name>` (or bind it: $mod+Ctrl+comma/period
#     in config/sway/config do left/right).
#
# Identifiers work here exactly as they do above, and for the same reason —
# so the DisplayLink screen must be named by identifier, not by DVI-I-1.

# Example, matching the three-screen layout above: comms on the laptop,
# everything else centre, media on the right, each falling back to the
# built-in panel when the externals are absent.

# workspace 1 output eDP-1
# workspace 2 output "MAKE MODEL SERIAL" eDP-1
# workspace 3 output "MAKE MODEL SERIAL" eDP-1
# workspace 9 output "MAKE MODEL SERIAL" eDP-1
